;; FH-INPUT-002 — the two lanes, and the SCOPE of the synchronous one.
;;
;; The door decides membership; this row is about what membership BUYS,
;; and — just as load-bearing — what it does not reach.
;;
;; A door site dispatches synchronously into the exact frame its commit
;; published, and then closes the pending window for the cells observing
;; THAT frame. Not the registry: a keystroke in one frame must never
;; force another frame's pending work to settle inside a listener it has
;; nothing to do with. The two-frame arrangement below is the whole
;; point — one frame is edited, the other is left pending, and the row
;; fails if either half moves.
{:fh/id "FH-INPUT-002"
 :fh/law
 "A door site dispatches SYNCHRONOUSLY into the exact frame its commit published and then flushes the cells observing that frame; a site outside the door takes the ordinary batched dispatcher into the same frame. The flush is frame-scoped: a pending cell observing a different frame is left pending, and no revision of it advances."

 :frames {:edited   :fh-input/frame-a
          :bystander :fh-input/frame-b}

 :query [:fh-input/field]
 :event :fh-input/edited
 :seed  {:field "re-"}
 :typed "re-frame"

 ;; The element half of the door verdict, as the walk supplies it.
 :door-site      {:tag :input :controlled? true :slot "onInput"}
 ;; One fact different, and the site falls out of the door — the same
 ;; event, the same frame, the ordinary lane.
 :batched-site   {:tag :input :controlled? true :slot "onBeforeInput"}

 :expected
 {:door-lane    :sync
  :batched-lane :async
  :dispatch-opts {:source :ui}

  ;; After the door site fires, still inside the call:
  :edited-frame-app-db {:field "re-frame"}

  ;; The scope, stated as the two halves that must BOTH hold.
  :edited-cell    {:dirty? false :revision-advanced? true}
  :bystander-cell {:dirty? true  :revision-advanced? false}}}
